{
 "fps": 30,
 "voice": "Piper en-US lessac (medium)",
 "lengthScale": 1.45,
 "cuts": [
  "s6-11",
  "s6-12",
  "s6-13"
 ],
 "parts": {
  "part1": {
   "title": "From Solid Balls to Plum Pudding",
   "titleFrames": 90,
   "totalFrames": 5547,
   "scenes": [
    {
     "id": "s1",
     "title": "Hook",
     "audio": "audio/part1-s1.flac",
     "startFrame": 90,
     "durationFrames": 1295,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s1-1",
       "text": "This pencil tip is made of carbon.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 2.73,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 82
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-2",
       "text": "Cut it smaller and smaller, down to single carbon atoms.",
       "start": 3.28,
       "end": 7.17,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 98,
       "endFrame": 215
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-3",
       "text": "“Atom” comes from the Greek word “atomos.” It means “can't be cut.”",
       "start": 8.52,
       "end": 13.37,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 256,
       "endFrame": 401
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-4",
       "text": "About 2,400 years ago, Leucippus and his student Democritus had an idea.",
       "start": 13.919999999999998,
       "end": 19.279999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 418,
       "endFrame": 578
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-5",
       "text": "Everything is made of atoms and empty space.",
       "start": 19.83,
       "end": 22.939999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 595,
       "endFrame": 688
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-6",
       "text": "Aristotle disagreed.",
       "start": 23.49,
       "end": 25.159999999999997,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 705,
       "endFrame": 755
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-7",
       "text": "He said everything is fire, air, earth, and water.",
       "start": 25.709999999999997,
       "end": 29.83,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 771,
       "endFrame": 895
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-8",
       "text": "Most people believed him for almost 2,000 years.",
       "start": 30.379999999999995,
       "end": 33.989999999999995,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 911,
       "endFrame": 1020
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-9",
       "text": "How can we learn what's inside something too small to see?",
       "start": 34.53999999999999,
       "end": 38.22999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1036,
       "endFrame": 1147
      },
      {
       "id": "s1-10",
       "text": "We do experiments: tests that check an idea.",
       "start": 38.779999999999994,
       "end": 42.16,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1163,
       "endFrame": 1265
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "s2",
     "title": "Dalton's Solid Sphere (1803)",
     "audio": "audio/part1-s2.flac",
     "startFrame": 1385,
     "durationFrames": 1487,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s2-1",
       "text": "By 1800, chemists knew two laws: rules nature always follows.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 5.97,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 179
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-2",
       "text": "First, Antoine Lavoisier. When chemicals react in a sealed jar, mass stays the same.",
       "start": 6.52,
       "end": 13.17,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 196,
       "endFrame": 395
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-3",
       "text": "Mass is how much stuff there is.",
       "start": 14.120000000000001,
       "end": 16.05,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 424,
       "endFrame": 482
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-4",
       "text": "Second, Joseph Proust. Water always has 8 grams of oxygen per gram of hydrogen.",
       "start": 16.6,
       "end": 22.51,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 498,
       "endFrame": 675
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-5",
       "text": "In 1803, John Dalton explained both laws.",
       "start": 23.060000000000002,
       "end": 26.880000000000003,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 692,
       "endFrame": 806
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-6",
       "text": "Atoms are tiny solid balls that can't be split.",
       "start": 27.430000000000003,
       "end": 30.870000000000005,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 823,
       "endFrame": 926
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-7",
       "text": "All atoms of one element, or pure substance, are alike.",
       "start": 31.42,
       "end": 35.45,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 943,
       "endFrame": 1064
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-8",
       "text": "Atoms join in whole numbers, never half an atom.",
       "start": 36.0,
       "end": 39.4,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1080,
       "endFrame": 1182
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-9",
       "text": "This is the Solid Sphere model: a picture that explains the clues.",
       "start": 40.45,
       "end": 44.93000000000001,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1214,
       "endFrame": 1348
      },
      {
       "id": "s2-10",
       "text": "It predicts that there's nothing inside an atom.",
       "start": 45.480000000000004,
       "end": 48.550000000000004,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1364,
       "endFrame": 1457
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "s3",
     "title": "Cathode Ray Tube → Plum Pudding (1897–1904)",
     "audio": "audio/part1-s3.flac",
     "startFrame": 2872,
     "durationFrames": 2675,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s3-1",
       "text": "In 1897, J. J. Thomson tested a cathode ray tube.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 4.56,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 137
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-2",
       "text": "It's a glass tube with most of the air pumped out.",
       "start": 5.109999999999999,
       "end": 8.129999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 153,
       "endFrame": 244
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-3",
       "text": "A glowing beam shoots from the negative end, called the cathode,",
       "start": 9.08,
       "end": 12.9,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 272,
       "endFrame": 387
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-4",
       "text": "to the positive end, called the anode.",
       "start": 13.05,
       "end": 15.49,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 392,
       "endFrame": 465
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-5",
       "text": "An object in the beam casts a sharp shadow.",
       "start": 16.04,
       "end": 19.07,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 481,
       "endFrame": 572
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-6",
       "text": "So the beam travels in straight lines.",
       "start": 19.619999999999997,
       "end": 21.919999999999998,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 589,
       "endFrame": 658
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-7",
       "text": "The beam bends toward a positive plate.",
       "start": 22.47,
       "end": 24.84,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 674,
       "endFrame": 745
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-8",
       "text": "Charge comes in plus and minus. Opposites pull together.",
       "start": 25.89,
       "end": 29.76,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 777,
       "endFrame": 893
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-9",
       "text": "So the beam is made of tiny negative bits, called particles.",
       "start": 30.310000000000002,
       "end": 34.0,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 909,
       "endFrame": 1020
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-10",
       "text": "A magnet bends the beam too.",
       "start": 34.550000000000004,
       "end": 37.11000000000001,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 1037,
       "endFrame": 1113
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-11",
       "text": "Thomson changed the metal, and the beam stayed the same.",
       "start": 37.660000000000004,
       "end": 41.050000000000004,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1130,
       "endFrame": 1232
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-12",
       "text": "So these particles are in every kind of atom.",
       "start": 42.0,
       "end": 44.65,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1260,
       "endFrame": 1340
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-13",
       "text": "And they're over 1,000 times lighter than a hydrogen atom.",
       "start": 45.2,
       "end": 48.93,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1356,
       "endFrame": 1468
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-14",
       "text": "He had discovered the electron.",
       "start": 49.480000000000004,
       "end": 51.53,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1484,
       "endFrame": 1546
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-15",
       "text": "Prediction: nothing inside. Result: smaller parts inside!",
       "start": 52.080000000000005,
       "end": 57.300000000000004,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1562,
       "endFrame": 1719
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-16",
       "text": "So the Solid Sphere model gets updated.",
       "start": 58.25000000000001,
       "end": 60.53000000000001,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1748,
       "endFrame": 1816
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-17",
       "text": "Whole atoms have no overall charge.",
       "start": 62.080000000000005,
       "end": 64.58000000000001,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1862,
       "endFrame": 1937
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-18",
       "text": "So something positive must balance the electrons.",
       "start": 65.13000000000001,
       "end": 68.4,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1954,
       "endFrame": 2052
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-19",
       "text": "In 1904, Thomson made the Plum Pudding model.",
       "start": 68.95000000000002,
       "end": 72.35000000000002,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2069,
       "endFrame": 2171
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-20",
       "text": "Electrons sit in a ball of spread-out positive charge.",
       "start": 72.90000000000002,
       "end": 76.42000000000002,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2187,
       "endFrame": 2293
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-21",
       "text": "Like blueberries in a muffin.",
       "start": 76.97000000000003,
       "end": 78.89000000000003,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2309,
       "endFrame": 2367
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-22",
       "text": "Plum pudding is an old British dessert.",
       "start": 79.44000000000003,
       "end": 81.51000000000002,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 2383,
       "endFrame": 2445
      },
      {
       "id": "s3-23",
       "text": "Next time, a gold foil experiment puts this model to the test.",
       "start": 82.06000000000003,
       "end": 86.65000000000003,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2462,
       "endFrame": 2600
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  "part2": {
   "title": "From Gold Foil to the Quantum Model",
   "titleFrames": 90,
   "totalFrames": 7870,
   "scenes": [
    {
     "id": "s4",
     "title": "Gold Foil → Nuclear Model (1909–1911)",
     "audio": "audio/part2-s4.flac",
     "startFrame": 90,
     "durationFrames": 3199,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s4-0",
       "text": "Last time, the atom went from a solid ball to plum pudding.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 4.64,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 139
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-1",
       "text": "Next, Ernest Rutherford's team tested the Plum Pudding model.",
       "start": 5.1899999999999995,
       "end": 9.5,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 156,
       "endFrame": 285
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-2",
       "text": "Hans Geiger and Ernest Marsden fired alpha particles at gold foil.",
       "start": 10.049999999999999,
       "end": 14.739999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 301,
       "endFrame": 442
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-3",
       "text": "Alpha particles are fast, heavy, and positive.",
       "start": 15.29,
       "end": 18.759999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 459,
       "endFrame": 563
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-4",
       "text": "They shoot out of radioactive material on their own.",
       "start": 19.31,
       "end": 22.64,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 579,
       "endFrame": 679
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-5",
       "text": "The gold foil was much thinner than paper.",
       "start": 23.189999999999998,
       "end": 25.72,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 696,
       "endFrame": 772
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-6",
       "text": "A screen around it flashed wherever a particle hit.",
       "start": 26.269999999999996,
       "end": 29.329999999999995,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 788,
       "endFrame": 880
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-7",
       "text": "They counted the flashes in a dark room, using a microscope.",
       "start": 29.879999999999995,
       "end": 33.699999999999996,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 896,
       "endFrame": 1011
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-8",
       "text": "Prediction: the positive charge is spread thin.",
       "start": 34.24999999999999,
       "end": 37.38999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1027,
       "endFrame": 1122
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-9",
       "text": "So every particle should zip straight through.",
       "start": 37.93999999999999,
       "end": 40.54999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1138,
       "endFrame": 1216
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-10",
       "text": "Here's what really happened.",
       "start": 41.89999999999999,
       "end": 43.709999999999994,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1257,
       "endFrame": 1311
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-11",
       "text": "Most particles went straight through.",
       "start": 44.25999999999999,
       "end": 46.63999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1328,
       "endFrame": 1399
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-12",
       "text": "A few bent at big angles.",
       "start": 47.48999999999999,
       "end": 49.389999999999986,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1425,
       "endFrame": 1482
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-13",
       "text": "And about 1 in 8,000 bounced almost straight back!",
       "start": 50.23999999999999,
       "end": 53.929999999999986,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1507,
       "endFrame": 1618
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-14",
       "text": "",
       "silent": true,
       "start": 54.77999999999999,
       "end": 57.77999999999999,
       "startFrame": 1643,
       "endFrame": 1733
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-15",
       "text": "Rutherford said it was like firing a giant shell at tissue paper,",
       "start": 57.77999999999999,
       "end": 61.58999999999999,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 1733,
       "endFrame": 1848
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-16",
       "text": "and having it bounce back and hit you!",
       "start": 61.73999999999999,
       "end": 63.80999999999999,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 1852,
       "endFrame": 1914
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-17",
       "text": "So the atom is mostly empty space.",
       "start": 64.35999999999999,
       "end": 67.10999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1931,
       "endFrame": 2013
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-18",
       "text": "In the middle is a tiny, positive center called the nucleus.",
       "start": 67.65999999999998,
       "end": 71.67999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2030,
       "endFrame": 2150
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-19",
       "text": "It holds almost all the mass. Electrons are outside it.",
       "start": 72.22999999999998,
       "end": 76.43999999999997,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2167,
       "endFrame": 2293
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-20",
       "text": "Make the nucleus a baseball, and the atom is 5 miles wide.",
       "start": 76.98999999999998,
       "end": 81.84999999999998,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 2310,
       "endFrame": 2455
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-21",
       "text": "Plum Pudding is updated. This is the Nuclear Model.",
       "start": 82.39999999999998,
       "end": 86.10999999999997,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 2472,
       "endFrame": 2583
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-22",
       "text": "Later, in 1932, came one more piece.",
       "start": 87.65999999999998,
       "end": 91.42999999999998,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 2630,
       "endFrame": 2743
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-23",
       "text": "Hydrogen has 1 proton, a positive particle in the nucleus.",
       "start": 91.97999999999999,
       "end": 96.47999999999999,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 2759,
       "endFrame": 2894
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-24",
       "text": "Helium has 2 protons, but 4 times the mass.",
       "start": 97.02999999999999,
       "end": 100.51999999999998,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 2911,
       "endFrame": 3016
      },
      {
       "id": "s4-25",
       "text": "James Chadwick found the missing mass: neutrons, with no charge.",
       "start": 101.07,
       "end": 105.61999999999999,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 3032,
       "endFrame": 3169
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "s5",
     "title": "Emission Spectra → Planetary Model (1913)",
     "audio": "audio/part2-s5.flac",
     "startFrame": 3289,
     "durationFrames": 2202,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s5-1",
       "text": "But where exactly are the electrons?",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 3.11,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 93
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-2",
       "text": "Physics said circling electrons should spiral into the nucleus.",
       "start": 3.6599999999999997,
       "end": 7.85,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 110,
       "endFrame": 236
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-3",
       "text": "The clue came from light.",
       "start": 8.4,
       "end": 9.82,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 252,
       "endFrame": 295
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-4",
       "text": "Hydrogen gas glows when electricity runs through it.",
       "start": 10.370000000000001,
       "end": 13.950000000000001,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 311,
       "endFrame": 419
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-5",
       "text": "A prism, a wedge of glass, splits that light into colors.",
       "start": 14.5,
       "end": 18.759999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 435,
       "endFrame": 563
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-6",
       "text": "White light makes a full rainbow.",
       "start": 19.31,
       "end": 21.24,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 579,
       "endFrame": 637
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-7",
       "text": "But hydrogen makes only four thin lines.",
       "start": 21.79,
       "end": 24.369999999999997,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 654,
       "endFrame": 731
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-8",
       "text": "Every element has its own lines, like a fingerprint.",
       "start": 24.919999999999998,
       "end": 28.339999999999996,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 748,
       "endFrame": 850
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-9",
       "text": "That's why metals make different colored flames.",
       "start": 28.889999999999997,
       "end": 32.04,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 867,
       "endFrame": 961
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-10",
       "text": "In 1913, Niels Bohr explained the lines.",
       "start": 32.589999999999996,
       "end": 36.08,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 978,
       "endFrame": 1082
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-11",
       "text": "Electrons can only be on certain levels, each with a fixed energy.",
       "start": 36.629999999999995,
       "end": 41.13999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1099,
       "endFrame": 1234
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-12",
       "text": "It's like stairs. You stand on a step, never between.",
       "start": 41.69,
       "end": 45.66,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1251,
       "endFrame": 1370
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-13",
       "text": "Add energy, and an electron jumps up a level.",
       "start": 46.21,
       "end": 49.160000000000004,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1386,
       "endFrame": 1475
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-14",
       "text": "When it falls back, it gives off one exact color.",
       "start": 49.71,
       "end": 53.52,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1491,
       "endFrame": 1606
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-15",
       "text": "A small drop, from level 3 to 2, gives red.",
       "start": 54.07,
       "end": 58.03,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1622,
       "endFrame": 1741
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-16",
       "text": "A bigger drop, from 5 to 2, gives violet: more energy.",
       "start": 59.18,
       "end": 64.49,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1775,
       "endFrame": 1935
      },
      {
       "id": "s5-17",
       "text": "Nuclear is updated. This is the Planetary Model: electrons on rings.",
       "start": 65.64,
       "end": 71.38,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1969,
       "endFrame": 2141
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "s6",
     "title": "The Quantum Model (1924–1927)",
     "audio": "audio/part2-s6.flac",
     "startFrame": 5491,
     "durationFrames": 1478,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s6-1",
       "text": "Bohr's model matched hydrogen perfectly.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 3.11,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 93
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-2",
       "text": "But it failed for atoms with more electrons, even helium.",
       "start": 3.6599999999999997,
       "end": 7.789999999999999,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 110,
       "endFrame": 234
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-3",
       "text": "In 1924, Louis de Broglie said electrons also act like waves.",
       "start": 8.34,
       "end": 13.27,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 250,
       "endFrame": 398
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-4",
       "text": "In 1926, Erwin Schrödinger wrote an equation treating electrons as waves.",
       "start": 13.82,
       "end": 19.2,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 415,
       "endFrame": 576
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-5",
       "text": "Its answers are orbitals: places an electron is likely to be.",
       "start": 19.75,
       "end": 24.03,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 592,
       "endFrame": 721
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-6",
       "text": "In 1927, Werner Heisenberg showed we can't know an electron's exact path.",
       "start": 24.58,
       "end": 30.45,
       "cut": true,
       "startFrame": 737,
       "endFrame": 914
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-7",
       "text": "Imagine 1,000 snapshots of where the electron is.",
       "start": 31.0,
       "end": 34.8,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 930,
       "endFrame": 1044
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-8",
       "text": "Stack them up, and you get a fuzzy cloud.",
       "start": 36.55,
       "end": 39.14,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1096,
       "endFrame": 1174
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-9",
       "text": "It's thickest where the electron is most likely to be.",
       "start": 40.089999999999996,
       "end": 43.419999999999995,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1203,
       "endFrame": 1303
      },
      {
       "id": "s6-10",
       "text": "Planetary is updated. This is the Quantum Model.",
       "start": 43.97,
       "end": 47.26,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 1319,
       "endFrame": 1418
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "s7",
     "title": "Recap",
     "audio": "audio/part2-s7.flac",
     "startFrame": 6969,
     "durationFrames": 901,
     "lines": [
      {
       "id": "s7-1",
       "text": "Five models. Each one changed because of evidence.",
       "start": 0.6,
       "end": 4.1499999999999995,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 18,
       "endFrame": 124
      },
      {
       "id": "s7-2",
       "text": "Two laws. Cathode rays. Gold foil. Light lines. Electron waves.",
       "start": 4.699999999999999,
       "end": 11.23,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 141,
       "endFrame": 337
      },
      {
       "id": "s7-3",
       "text": "Each model made a prediction. An experiment tested it.",
       "start": 12.379999999999999,
       "end": 15.919999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 371,
       "endFrame": 478
      },
      {
       "id": "s7-4",
       "text": "When the result didn't fit, scientists updated the model.",
       "start": 16.47,
       "end": 20.0,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 494,
       "endFrame": 600
      },
      {
       "id": "s7-5",
       "text": "Each experiment didn't throw science away.",
       "start": 20.549999999999997,
       "end": 23.139999999999997,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 616,
       "endFrame": 694
      },
      {
       "id": "s7-6",
       "text": "It made our picture of the atom better.",
       "start": 23.689999999999998,
       "end": 26.029999999999998,
       "cut": false,
       "startFrame": 711,
       "endFrame": 781
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 }
}